{
  // **Nobody's deployment reads this file as it stands, and nobody should edit it to make one.**
  //
  // It is the shape of the configuration, shared by everybody, holding only what is true of
  // every deployment. What differs between one deployment and the next — database id, bucket
  // name, hostname, sender address — is absent on purpose, and stays absent: `scripts/deploy.ts`
  // merges those in at build time from Workers Builds' build variables, in whichever Cloudflare
  // account is doing the building. That is the same for the official deployment and for
  // somebody self-hosting; see docs/deployment.md.
  //
  // So this file has two readers: `npm run dev` and `wrangler dev`, which need no account of
  // anybody's, and the generator, which reads it as its starting point.
  //
  // **Editing it to hold your own ids is the one thing not to do.** Not because it would fail
  // — it would work — but because upstream keeps changing these lines, so every `git pull`
  // would conflict here, on a fork of a public repository. Build variables cost nothing and
  // that cost is permanent.
  //
  // Three things checked against wrangler 4.124 rather than inferred from the docs, which
  // together are why the generator exists at all:
  //
  //  - This file is valid as it stands. Wrangler raises nothing about the missing ids, the
  //    missing `routes` or the missing `vars` — it gets as far as bundling, so an error here
  //    is a real error and not the blanks.
  //  - Wrangler does not interpolate `${VAR}` in a configuration file.
  //  - `wrangler deploy` has no flag that overrides a D1 `database_id`, an R2 `bucket_name`
  //    or a KV `id`, so a command line cannot stand in for them either.
  //
  // Leaving an id out and letting wrangler provision the resource is not a way round it
  // either. Wrangler writes the id back into the config, which works from a laptop and does
  // not work from a build environment, because that environment cannot commit to the
  // repository. The id is lost, and every later deploy tries to create the resource again and
  // fails with "already exists" (code 10014). This project has been there.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "tidemarks",
  "main": "worker/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-01",
  // Stated rather than left at wrangler's 8787: the Vite dev server proxies /api and /auth to
  // this port, and that target is written in vite.config.ts. The two have to agree, so the
  // number belongs in a file rather than in whatever each of us types.
  "dev": {
    "port": 5002
  },
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application",
    // Everything the Worker owns has to be listed here. `single-page-application` means an
    // unmatched path serves index.html, so a missing entry does not 404 — it answers /mcp
    // with the React app, and the failure reads as "this MCP server speaks HTML".
    "run_worker_first": ["/api/*", "/auth/*", "/mcp", "/authorize", "/oauth/*", "/.well-known/*"]
  },

  // No `routes`, and none to add here. A custom domain comes from the CF_ROUTE build
  // variable, and leaving that unset is a supported deployment rather than an incomplete one:
  // the Worker is then served from `<name>.workers.dev`, which is what makes a fresh
  // deployment usable before anybody has bought a hostname.

  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      // `database_name` and `database_id` both arrive from build variables (CF_D1_NAME,
      // CF_D1_ID). The name here is only what `wrangler dev --local` calls its local copy.
      "database_name": "tidemarks",
      // Stated rather than left to the default so a reader can find where the schema lives.
      // `npm run deploy` applies these before uploading the Worker, which is the order that
      // matters: a Worker reading a column the database has not got yet fails on the first
      // request, not at deploy time.
      "migrations_dir": "./migrations"
    }
  ],
  "r2_buckets": [
    {
      "binding": "BUCKET",
      "bucket_name": "tidemarks"
    }
  ],
  // OAuth grants, authorization codes and access tokens for the MCP server. KV rather than D1
  // because workers-oauth-provider owns the storage and only speaks KV; nothing in here is a
  // reader's data, and every entry expires on its own.
  "kv_namespaces": [
    {
      "binding": "OAUTH_KV"
      // The id comes from the CF_KV_ID build variable. This is the binding the 10014 story at
      // the top of this file is about.
    }
  ]

  // No `vars`, and that is not an oversight. Three values belong here, all from build
  // variables, and none of them has a default that is right for anybody else:
  //
  //   RP_ID      the hostname passkeys are scoped to (CF_RP_ID). **Permanently locked once the
  //              first passkey is registered** — changing it invalidates every passkey.
  //   ORIGIN     "https://" + that hostname (CF_ORIGIN).
  //   MAIL_FROM  sender of the magic-code mail, on a domain Resend has verified
  //              (CF_MAIL_FROM). **Not necessarily the hostname above** — Resend only knows
  //              about the domain you verified with it, and refuses every message from any
  //              other. Optional: unset, and without RESEND_API_KEY, the login code is written
  //              to the log (`npx wrangler tail`) rather than mailed, which is the whole
  //              zero-vendor path. See worker/email.ts.
  //
  // A plausible-looking placeholder would be worse than nothing here, which is the other
  // reason they are not in this file: two of the three fail silently. A deployment carrying
  // somebody else's RP_ID deploys fine and cannot be logged into, and one with an unverified
  // MAIL_FROM deploys fine and mails nothing.
  //
  // Whether RP_ID should fall back to the request's host is an open question — issue #9.
  //
  // "OPEN_SIGNUP": "true" — absent, and absent means only addresses in the `signup_allowlist`
  // table can create an account. Adding this line is the launch line itself (ADR-0004): the
  // moment data starts belonging to people who did not agree it could be thrown away. It is a
  // deploy on purpose, so that opening signup leaves a commit behind, while adding one friend
  // to the allowlist is a `wrangler d1 execute` and needs nothing.

  // secrets (npx wrangler secret put): COOKIE_SECRET, RESEND_API_KEY. Secrets, unlike the
  // values above, are attached to the Worker and read at runtime — build variables are not
  // visible to a running Worker, which is exactly right for values whose job is to write this
  // file.
  //
  // COOKIE_SECRET is required — the Worker deploys without it and then nobody can log in.
  // RESEND_API_KEY unset means magic codes go to the log instead of the mail, as above.
}
